Для работы утилиты требуется предварительная установка (обычно через pip install xlmdeobfuscator).
xlmdeobfuscator is an open-source, Python-based automation tool used by security analysts to deobfuscate and analyze 
malicious Excel 4.0 macros (also known as XLM macros).xlmdeobfuscator was created as a direct response to a specific
threat: hidden and heavily obfuscated formulas embedded inside old Excel document formats.

What Problem Does This Tool Solve?
Between 2018 and 2021, threat actors began heavily utilizing legacy Excel 4.0 (XLM) macros instead of standard VBA to
bypass antivirus detection. Unlike VBA macros—which run inside a separate code module—XLM macros execute directly inside
the cells of the spreadsheet itself using formulas like =EXEC(...) or =CALL(...).
Attackers obfuscate this malicious code using several tricks:
Scattering commands across completely hidden, locked, or out-of-sight cells.
Encrypting strings via mathematical formulas (e.g., reconstructing a malware download URL letter-by-letter 
using the =CHAR() function).
Dynamically assembling the payload code only at the exact moment the file is opened by the victim.
Standard static parsers only see a chaotic mess of random numbers and formulas. xlmdeobfuscator solves this via emulation.
It runs an internal engine that safely "plays back" the macro execution inside an isolated memory space without ever
opening Microsoft Excel, decrypting all hidden values on the fly to reveal the attacker's true intentions.

🔎 Key Capabilities of xlmdeobfuscatorFormula Emulation: 
It steps through mathematical cell calculations sequentially, uncovering strings hidden behind functions 
like CHAR(), RUN(), or CONCATENATE().
Indicator of Compromise (IOC) Extraction: The tool automatically pulls out hidden URLs, malicious IP addresses, 
file paths, and executed PowerShell/CMD commands from the cells.
Multi-Format Support: It works seamlessly with .xls (Excel 97-2003), .xlsm (OpenXML Macro-Enabled Spreadsheet), 
and .xlsb (Excel Binary Format) files.
API Integration: It can be operated natively as a command-line tool or imported as a Python library into automated
malware analysis pipelines and email sandboxes.

    Command Cheat Sheet
1. Baseline File Analysis
The most common command to run an automatic emulation pass and print a readable log of actions:

        xlmdeobfuscator -f suspicious_invoice.xlsm

2. Output Results as JSON
If you are automating your analysis and need to feed the discovered data (URLs/commands) directly into another system 
like a SIEM or SOAR platform:

      xlmdeobfuscator -f suspicious_invoice.xlsm -j

3. Limit Emulation Depth
By default, the tool tries to emulate the entire process. If a macro utilizes an infinite loop 
(a common anti-analysis technique to freeze tools), you can hard-cap the number of steps:

      xlmdeobfuscator -f suspicious_invoice.xlsm --max-stc-steps 2000
